---
import "../styles/paper.css";
import "katex/dist/katex.min.css";
import katex from "katex";

const k = (tex: string) => katex.renderToString(tex, { throwOnError: true });
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Two players claim cells of an n by n grid; the first to own four corners of a square wins. The first player wins exactly when n is at least 5, by a machine-checked strategy. Play against it."
    />
  </head>
  <body>
    <header class="masthead">
      <h1>The Square Achievement Game on n × n Boards</h1>
      <p class="byline">Sean Reid · August 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="abstract">
      <strong>Abstract.</strong> Two players alternately claim cells of an
      <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid; the first to hold all four corners
      of an axis-aligned square wins. We show the first player has a winning strategy
      precisely when <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />, and that all smaller
      boards are drawn under optimal play. The strategy is explicit, and the
      searches that verify it are formalized in Lean 4, so each verified board
      size comes with a machine-checked proof.
    </p>

    <figure class="game">
      <sag-board n="5"></sag-board>
      <figcaption>
        Figure 1. Play the game: the bot moves first, replaying the strategy
        below move for move, and you reply. Change n to explore; below 5 × 5
        neither side can force a win.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The game</h2>
    <p>
      Played on the cells of an <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid, P1 moving
      first. A winning set is any
      <span
        set:html={k("\\{(r,c),\\,(r,c{+}s),\\,(r{+}s,c),\\,(r{+}s,c{+}s)\\}")}
      /> with <span set:html={k("s \\ge 1")} />: the four corners of an
      axis-aligned square, from 2 × 2 blocks up. A full board with no completed
      square is a draw. Tilted squares do not count. Both players are after the
      same squares, so even a player who cannot win anymore can still block, and
      on the boards below 5 × 5 blocking is enough: neither player can force a
      win.
    </p>

    <figure class="diagram">
      <svg
        viewBox="-4 -4 152 152"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A 6 by 6 grid showing the corners of a 2 by 2 block and of a square of side 3"
      >
        <defs>
          <pattern
            id="grid6"
            width="24"
            height="24"
            patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
          >
            <path
              d="M24 0H0V24"
              fill="none"
              stroke="var(--rule)"
              stroke-width="0.8"
              opacity="0.5"></path>
          </pattern>
        </defs>
        <rect width="144" height="144" fill="url(#grid6)"></rect>
        <rect
          width="144"
          height="144"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--rule)"
          stroke-width="0.8"
          opacity="0.5"></rect>
        <rect
          x="12"
          y="108"
          width="24"
          height="24"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--accent)"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          stroke-dasharray="4 3"></rect>
        <rect
          x="60"
          y="12"
          width="72"
          height="72"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--accent)"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          stroke-dasharray="4 3"></rect>
        <circle cx="12" cy="108" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="36" cy="108" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="12" cy="132" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="36" cy="132" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="60" cy="12" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="132" cy="12" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="60" cy="84" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
        <circle cx="132" cy="84" r="4" fill="var(--ink)"></circle>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        Figure 2. Winning shapes: the corners of a 2 × 2 block and of a square
        of side 3. Only the corners must be held.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="theorem">
      <span class="label">Theorem 1.</span> The first player has a winning strategy
      in the square achievement game on the
      <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> board if and only if
      <span set:html={k("n \\ge 5")} />.
    </div>

    <h2>History</h2>
    <p>
      Harary called games like this achievement games: both players build toward
      the same target, and the first to finish wins
      <a class="cite" href="#ref-1">[1]</a>. Erickson asked what happens for the
      axis-aligned square on an
      <span set:html={k("n \\times n")} /> grid
      <a class="cite" href="#ref-2">[2]</a>, and the question is recorded on the
      Open Problem Garden <a class="cite" href="#ref-3">[3]</a>. Two ends of the
      answer were known. Jenrich reported computer searches giving draws on 3 ×
      3 and 4 × 4 and a first-player win on 5 × 5
      <a class="cite" href="#ref-4">[4]</a>. At the other end, Bacher and
      Eliahou proved that every two-coloring of a 15 × 15 grid contains a
      monochromatic square
      <a class="cite" href="#ref-5">[5]</a>, so no game on a board of size 15 or
      more can end in a draw; a standard strategy-stealing argument then gives
      the first player a win, though it produces no strategy. In between, from 6
      × 6 through 14 × 14, the outcome was open. We close that range, and the
      proof is a strategy you can watch: the bot in Figure 1 is playing it.
    </p>

    <h2>The strategy</h2>
    <p>
      On every turn the strategy checks two reflexes before anything else:
      complete one of your own squares if you can; otherwise, if the opponent is
      one cell from completing theirs, block it. What remains is choosing the
      quiet moves, and those come from a fixed lookup table. The strategy opens
      at (2,2), answers the opponent's first reply with a tabled second move,
      and the first two replies together select a plan: a short list of cells
      played in order.
    </p>
    <p>
      Plans are forcing. Each plan move puts three corners of some square in
      place, so the opponent must block at once or lose, until one move creates
      two threats and no single block answers both. The opponent's forced blocks
      pile up too, and sometimes complete three corners of their own square; the
      block reflex answers those counter-threats, and the search that generated
      the table only accepted plans that keep the initiative through such
      exchanges.
    </p>
    <p>
      One table serves every board from 6 × 6 up, for two reasons. First, its
      plans never leave the 6 × 6 corner region of the board, so every square
      the strategy builds exists on every board containing that region. Second,
      a reply far from the corner region can join a square through two of the
      strategy's stones only from the region's own rows and columns, so far
      replies are looked up by geometric class rather than by exact position.
      And a pair of far replies can combine against a single cell of the corner
      region; such pairs get entries of their own, keyed by that cell and built
      to never hand it over. The 5 × 5 board is the odd one out: it is too
      cramped for pure forcing, and exhaustive analysis shows that no
      all-forcing table wins there. Its table comes instead from a full solution
      of the 5 × 5 game and includes quiet moves that fork on the opponent's
      reply.
    </p>

    <h2>Why believe a computer search</h2>
    <p>
      For each board size the proof is an exhaustive search: the strategy plays
      against every sequence of replies, which at 9 × 9 means more than three
      million positions. Searches of this kind have gone wrong before, in this
      very game. The first published search for 5 × 5 contained an error and had
      to be corrected <a class="cite" href="#ref-4">[4]</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      We therefore wrote the search three times with nothing shared between
      them: once in Python, used while discovering the tables; once in Lean; and
      once in TypeScript, driving the bot on this page. The three agree on every
      board size we have tried, which is reassuring but is not the proof. The
      proof rests on the Lean version alone. It comes with a soundness theorem,
      itself machine-checked, which says that whenever the checker answers true,
      a winning strategy tree exists for the game as defined mathematically.
      Nothing about how the tables were found has to be taken on faith; they sit
      in the development as plain data, and a wrong table would fail the check
      rather than slip through it.
    </p>

    <h2>Toward every board at once</h2>
    <p>
      For boards of size 15 and up we know the first player wins, but not how:
      no game there can end in a draw, and the second player cannot be the one
      with a winning strategy, so the first player is. Nothing in that argument
      says which move to make. We expect the table strategy above to win on
      every board of size 6 and up, and proving it would give one explicit
      strategy for all of them. What makes this hard is the room a large board
      gives the opponent. Stones placed far from the corner region belong to
      squares that exist only out there, and whenever one of those squares
      reaches three corners, the strategy has to spend a move on it. The table
      was generated and checked on a board of size 15; a larger board can steer
      it into positions its plans were never checked against. Figure 3 shows the
      smallest example we know from an earlier version of the table. Everything
      through 15 × 15 is settled unconditionally, a faster but unproven replica
      of the checker verifies the current table through 17 × 17, and the general
      statement is work we have not finished.
    </p>
    <figure class="diagram">
      <svg
        viewBox="-8 -24 356 352"
        role="img"
        aria-label="A 16 by 16 board with a farm square through the corner region and three far opponent stones"
      >
        <defs>
          <pattern
            id="grid16"
            width="20"
            height="20"
            patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse"
          >
            <path
              d="M20 0H0V20"
              fill="none"
              stroke="var(--rule)"
              stroke-width="0.7"
              opacity="0.45"></path>
          </pattern>
        </defs>
        <rect width="320" height="320" fill="url(#grid16)"></rect>
        <rect
          width="320"
          height="320"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--rule)"
          stroke-width="0.7"
          opacity="0.45"></rect>
        <rect width="120" height="120" fill="var(--accent)" fill-opacity="0.09"
        ></rect>
        <rect
          x="70"
          y="10"
          width="240"
          height="240"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--accent)"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          stroke-dasharray="5 4"></rect>
        <circle
          cx="70"
          cy="10"
          r="6"
          fill="var(--paper)"
          stroke="var(--ink)"
          stroke-width="1.5"></circle>
        <circle
          cx="310"
          cy="10"
          r="6"
          fill="var(--paper)"
          stroke="var(--ink)"
          stroke-width="1.5"></circle>
        <circle
          cx="70"
          cy="250"
          r="6"
          fill="var(--paper)"
          stroke="var(--ink)"
          stroke-width="1.5"></circle>
        <circle
          cx="310"
          cy="250"
          r="8"
          fill="none"
          stroke="var(--ink)"
          stroke-width="1.5"
          stroke-dasharray="4 3"></circle>
        <text
          x="70"
          y="-8"
          text-anchor="middle"
          font-size="14"
          fill="var(--ink)"
          font-style="italic">t</text
        >
        <text
          x="310"
          y="-8"
          text-anchor="middle"
          font-size="14"
          fill="var(--ink)"
          font-style="italic">o1</text
        >
        <text
          x="52"
          y="255"
          text-anchor="end"
          font-size="14"
          fill="var(--ink)"
          font-style="italic">o2</text
        >
        <text
          x="328"
          y="255"
          text-anchor="start"
          font-size="14"
          fill="var(--ink)"
          font-style="italic">z</text
        >
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        Figure 3. A farm on the 16 × 16 board. The opponent's far replies o1 and
        o2 align with the corner region (shaded); once a forced block lands on
        t, the dashed square threatens at the dashed cell z. The square does not
        fit any smaller board.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Formalization and downloads</h2>
    <p>
      The proof is formalized in Lean 4
      <a class="cite" href="#ref-6">[6]</a>, building on mathlib
      <a class="cite" href="#ref-7">[7]</a>: the game semantics, an executable
      verifier with a machine-checked soundness theorem, and the strategy tables
      as certificates; the assembly for arbitrary board size is in progress.
      Both artifacts are built from this repository on every deploy:
      <a href="downloads/square-achievement-paper.pdf">the paper (PDF)</a> and
      <a href="downloads/square-achievement-formalization.tar.gz"
        >the Lean development (tar.gz)</a
      >.
    </p>

